1-3-12-rate-these-names.prompt:
Everything you need is in 1.3.6, 1.3.7, 1.3.8 and the worked example in 1.3.10: go back and reread them if you get stuck. A sentence per name is plenty, in your own everyday words.

Here are six variable names. For each one, say whether it is a good name or a bad one, and why: name the rule it follows or breaks. (1.3.6, 1.3.7 and 1.3.8)

  let x = 5;
  let numberOfStudents = 5;
  let d = "Monday";
  let currentDay = "Monday";
  let temp = 98.6;
  let bodyTemperature = 98.6;

Then answer two more:

7. One of those six is genuinely arguable rather than clearly good or clearly bad. Which one, and what makes it arguable? (1.3.8)

8. Match each of these four names: temp, x, a1, myVariable, to its problem: too generic, an abbreviation with no context, a numbered name that says nothing, and meaningless but harmless-sounding. (1.3.7)

1-7-2-ch1-individual-pa-own-words.prompt:
This is a closed-book chapter test sat individually in class. Mark three short answers written in the student's own words. Two or three sentences per answer is a complete answer; do not reward length.

QUESTION 1 -- Umbrella activities. Chapter 1 (1.1.10) split the work into framework activities and umbrella activities. The student must say what makes an activity an umbrella activity -- that it runs ALONGSIDE the framework activities across the whole project rather than happening at one point in it -- name one. The six this course taught in 1.1.10 are training/communication, risk management, configuration management, quality management, architecture management and security management -- accept any of those six, and accept a close paraphrase of one (a student who writes "version control" for configuration management, or "quality assurance" for quality management, has named the right thing). Accept a real umbrella activity outside that six only if the student also shows it runs throughout, and say what would go wrong on a project that skipped it. Naming an umbrella activity without the 'runs throughout' idea is partial. Naming a FRAMEWORK activity (inception, elaboration, construction, deployment) instead is missing.

QUESTION 2 -- Reading a program that has more than one real problem. The student was shown this:

  // set x
  let x = 12.5;
  // set y
  let y = 3;
  // multiply
  let z = x * x;
  console.log(z);

It runs without crashing, but z is WRONG: the multiplication uses x * x instead of x * y, a copy-paste-shaped operand mistake, so it prints 156.25 instead of the correct 37.5. The student must name TWO things wrong with it and say what each costs. At least one has to be about the comments. There are (at least) four legitimate targets and any two count, as long as one is the comments -- different students may reasonably pick different pairs, and that is by design:

  (a) the calculation is wrong -- x * x instead of x * y. Credit this ONLY if the student actually identifies that the multiplication uses the wrong value/variable (or otherwise correctly names what is wrong with the arithmetic) -- a vague 'the math might be off' guess with no reason given is NOT this criterion met, mark it partial or missing.
  (b) the names x, y and z say nothing about what the values mean (1.3.6, 1.3.7)
  (c) the comments restate the syntax rather than explaining why, so they add nothing a reader could not see (1.3.4)
  (d) the printed number has no label, so the output does not say what it is

Credit any specific, correct fault with a real consequence. Do NOT credit 'it needs more comments' on its own -- the fault is that these comments say nothing, and more of them would be worse. Do NOT require the student to find (a) -- two of (b)/(c)/(d), with (c) among them, is a complete answer.

QUESTION 3 -- High-level and low-level. The student must distinguish the two by distance from the hardware: a high-level language reads closer to human language and hides how the machine works, a low-level language is closer to what the processor actually does and the programmer manages more of it themselves (1.4.5). Then one real reason to choose the lower-level one: speed, tight control over memory, small devices with little to spare, writing something an operating system or driver needs. A reason that amounts to 'it is more professional' is missing.

Mark each answer against its own criterion only. This is a test: report what the student wrote, do not coach.
